WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.033
Async endpoints.

2
00:00:02.333 --> 00:00:04.314
An LLM call is mostly waiting.

3
00:00:04.314 --> 00:00:09.133
How you wait decides whether one server handles two users or two hundred.

4
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:14.933
An async endpoint can serve other requests while it waits on input and output.

5
00:00:15.233 --> 00:00:21.467
An LLM call is the purest wait there is: five to thirty seconds of just listening to a socket.

6
00:00:21.767 --> 00:00:27.100
Every await hands control back to the event loop, so one worker holds hundreds of requests.

7
00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:29.800
Let's follow one request.

8
00:00:30.100 --> 00:00:31.700
Request A calls the LLM.

9
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.833
At the await, it steps aside.

10
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:36.500
The event loop serves other users meanwhile.

11
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.733
When the answer arrives, A resumes.

12
00:00:39.933 --> 00:00:44.500
Compare two versions of one endpoint under twenty simultaneous requests.

13
00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.300
Blocking: the calls line up, and twenty times three is about sixty seconds.

14
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.400
Awaiting: the waits overlap, and the batch takes about three seconds.

15
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:55.800
So here is the rule.

16
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.193
Skip the requests library.

17
00:00:58.193 --> 00:01:00.367
Use the async httpx client.

18
00:01:00.667 --> 00:01:01.867
Skip time sleep.

19
00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:03.367
Await asyncio sleep.

20
00:01:03.667 --> 00:01:09.433
Blocking work belongs in a plain def route, which runs in a threadpool, or behind a task queue.

21
00:01:09.733 --> 00:01:13.400
Inside an async route, everything that waits must be awaitable.

22
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.067
Here are both endpoints.

23
00:01:16.367 --> 00:01:18.891
The frozen one sleeps with a blocking call.

24
00:01:18.891 --> 00:01:20.300
Nobody else gets served.

25
00:01:20.600 --> 00:01:24.833
The fine one awaits, so other requests proceed during those three seconds.

26
00:01:26.033 --> 00:01:27.867
Here's a story that happens a lot.

27
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:33.715
The first chat endpoint used the synchronous requests library inside an async route.

28
00:01:33.715 --> 00:01:35.367
Solo testing was perfect.

29
00:01:35.667 --> 00:01:38.326
On demo day, twelve people asked at once.

30
00:01:38.326 --> 00:01:42.867
Each eight second call froze the loop, and the last request timed out.

31
00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:45.052
One dependency swap fixed it.

32
00:01:45.052 --> 00:01:48.433
The same box now holds two hundred concurrent chats.

33
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:50.456
Let's recap.

34
00:01:50.456 --> 00:01:53.267
Async lets one worker overlap many waits.

35
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:56.067
A blocking call freezes the whole event loop.

36
00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:59.033
Inside an async route, use awaitable IO.

37
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:02.740
And put blocking work in a plain def route or a queue.

38
00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:04.633
Next up: dependency injection.
